			},
			"content": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": "Trusted Types"
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"type": "paragraph",
			"content": [
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": "The viewer and plugins operate under a Trusted Types policy ("
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": "require-trusted-types-for 'script'",
					"marks": [
						{
							"type": "code"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": ") in Chromium: DOM-sink usage in core and the SDK goes through a policy or safe APIs. Core installs a pass-through "
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": "default",
					"marks": [
						{
							"type": "code"
						},
						{
							"type": "bold"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": " Trusted Types policy (untrusted HTML is sanitized upstream before it reaches any DOM sink) — if your app already installs its own "
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": "default",
					"marks": [
						{
							"type": "code"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": " policy, core defers to it. Svelte creates its own "
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"marks": [
						{
							"type": "code"
						}
					],
					"text": "svelte-trusted-html"
				},
				{
					"type": "text",
					"text": " policy at module load, so that name has to be allowed too or the viewer never mounts. Every bundle that embeds Svelte creates it again — the element IIFE and a plugin IIFE are two — and a repeated policy name throws without 'allow-duplicates'. If you enforce Trusted Types, include it alongside the policy above:"
				}
			],
			"attrs": {
				"id": "cd147c00-df45-4650-b269-48b8a0685be0"
			}
		},
		{
			"type": "codeBlock",
			"attrs": {
				"language": "plaintext",
